Médano Skronk
Ingredients: FREE JAZZ / AVANT-GARDE + REGGAETÓN & LATIN RAP + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Vocoder  
Creative Catalyst: It emerged from a desert caravan culture that trades in salvaged synthesizers.

Médano Skronk sounds like an illegal late-night sound clash at an arid desert checkpoint: the syncopated, sub-bass bounce of classic dembow and fast Latin rap flows are ambushed by microtonal, shrieking free-jazz saxophones and hallucinated synthesizers salvaged from abandoned radar posts. Voices do not sing; they mutate through sand-dusted vocoders into mechanical, spectral androids chanting street anthems over chaotic, polyrhythmic horn explosions and tape-saturated delay trails.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 92–98 BPM. The unmistakable syncopated tresillo kick-and-snare swagger of classic reggaetón serves as the anchor, but it constantly stutters, trips over rubato horn interruptions, and shifts between heavy, locked sub-bass stomps and unmetered free-jazz blowouts.
- Harmonic Palette: Dissonant and modal. Atonal cluster chords and overblown multiphonics meet Arabic and Andalusian quarter-tone scales, microtonally altered by dying voltage regulators and uncalibrated synthesizer oscillators.
- Production Signatures: Vocoded Spanish and dialect rap processed through spring reverbs and ring modulators; screeching tenor saxophone overtones fed as carrier signals into vintage vocoders; Dembow drum kits sampled directly onto crusty 12-bit samplers powered by fluctuating solar-inverter rigs, yielding gritty harmonic grit and warbling tape flutter.
- Dynamics: Violent pendulum swings. Tracks often drop from deafening, dense walls of atonal brass skronk and blown-out 808 sub-bass into bone-dry, whispery vocoder rap backed only by a clicking hi-hat and the hiss of desert wind caught in ribbon mics.

Scene & Origin Lore
The sound crystallized among the wandering lithium-and-scrap merchants who traverse the barren salt scrub between northern Chile, the Bolivian altiplano, and southern Peru. Traveling in retrofitted flatbed convoys running on biodiesel, these traders haul discarded mid-century electronics, broken Japanese FM synthesizers, and vintage broadcast transmitters across remote border corridors. At desert waystations called tambos, evening feasts around communal fire pits organically evolved into open-circuit jam sessions. Self-taught woodwind players, raised on Aymara brass bands and Albert Ayler bootlegs, began dueling with streetwise Latin rappers who ran their rapid-fire verses through re-wired Roland and Korg vocoders powered by truck batteries. Dressed in utilitarian canvas dust duster coats patched with silver circuit-trace fabric, mirrored welding goggles, and alpaca wool scarves, the scene’s acolytes celebrate the friction between raw human breath and resurrected microchips.

Typical Instruments Used:
Salvaged vocoders (Roland VP-330, Korg VC-10 modded for battery power), prepared tenor and baritone saxophones, battered Roland TR-808 and TR-08 rhythm modules, modified Casio CZ and Yamaha FM synthesizers, tape-loop machines, cuícas, and congas fitted with contact microphones.

For Fans Of: Arca, Shabaka Hutchings, Peter Brötzmann, Calle 13 (at their most experimental).

