Track 3: "¿Quién apagó la paila?"
- Story & Context: The legendary myth piece of the caravan tells of the night a flash freezing wind killed the hearth fires across the camp, leaving forty hungry tradespeople shivering in the dark until Pato began pounding out a syncopated Latin bounce on empty steel kerosene barrels to stave off frostbite. Ximena joined in on a cracked wooden flute wrapped in duct tape, while Mateo screamed into an unpowered telephone receiver wired directly into a salvaged modulator, delivering the iconic desert refrain: "El viento no baila con nadie pero siempre cobra la entrada." By dawn, the entire caravan was dancing in a delirious, sleep-deprived circle around cold grease and dead ash.
